          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years</strong><br>\nJohannes Brahms was born on May 7, 1833, in Hamburg, Germany. He demonstrated musical talent at a young age, receiving piano lessons from his father and later from other notable teachers. Brahms was also exposed to a wide range of music, which included both classical and folk music traditions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to Vienna</strong><br>\nIn his early 20s, Brahms settled in Vienna, Austria, which was the heart of the classical music world. There, he became a leading figure in the musical scene. Vienna served as the launchpad for his career, and he would call it home for the rest of his life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Musical Style</strong><br>\nBrahms was a traditionalist who drew inspiration from composers like Beethoven and Schubert. Yet, he brought a fresh perspective to the classical forms. His compositions include symphonies, concerti, chamber music, piano works, and over 200 songs.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
