          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Late Works</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Brahms continued to compose music that was increasingly reflective and introspective. His final pieces, such as the clarinet quintet and piano pieces, are considered some of his most emotionally powerful works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nBrahms passed away from cancer on April 3, 1897, in Vienna. He left behind a legacy as one of the greatest composers of the 19th century. His works remain staples of the concert repertoire, and his influence on subsequent generations of composers has been profound.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Brahms</strong><br>\nToday, Johannes Brahms is celebrated as a master of symphonic and sonata style music. His ability to synthesize the classical and romantic traditions in his music has ensured his status as one of the towering figures in Western art music.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
